    "Amazon": {revenue:73, total:717, buyer:true, people:0, profit:0, conf:"derived",
      scope:"2025 hardware purchases: capitalized servers, networking and storage for AWS and Amazon's other businesses (accrual basis, including finance leases and unpaid capex), plus consumer-device procurement (Echo, Kindle, Fire, Ring), which is cost of sales, not capex. Excludes land, buildings, power/cooling (heavy equipment), fulfillment and logistics equipment, and Kuiper launches. Also includes the chip-design EDA and IP licences shown, for its in-house chips.", period:"Calendar 2025 (FY ended Dec 31, 2025)",
      otherLabel:"Other hardware suppliers (Accton, Celestica, Seagate/WDC, optics, Alchip, ...)",
      outside:[{label:"Cloud, retail & advertising customers", usd:73}],
      basis:"Amazon 2025 10-K: purchases of P&E $131.8B + $2.9B finance-lease additions = $134.7B; accrual net additions $142.4B (AWS $96.5B). Gross 'servers and networking equipment' rose from $113.2B to $172.5B (+$59.3B). Disposals were not small: $41.9B of D&A against a $35.7B rise in accumulated depreciation implies at least ~$6B retired across P&E. Server retirements of ~$3-4B are partly offset by ~$1-2B of FX translation gains on foreign servers, for ~+$1.5B net. Adding ~30% (~$7.5B, an assumption) of the $25.1B construction-in-progress rise as IT gear not yet in service gives ~$68B of accrual IT hardware: ~48% of accrual additions, or ~$63B in cash after the $10.2B rise in unpaid capex. This excludes land/buildings (+$32.1B), heavy power/cooling equipment (+$13.3B) and fulfillment equipment (+$9.9B). Adding ~$5B of device procurement (unit-based estimate: Echo, Fire TV, Fire tablets, Kindle, Ring/Blink) gives ~$73B (plausible range ~$65-80B). Tracked suppliers account for $55.8B (76%), leaving about $17.2B for other hardware suppliers.",
      sources:[{title:"Amazon 2025 Form 10-K: Note 1 (useful lives, heavy/other equipment definitions), Note 3 Property and Equipment (gross P&E by category, accumulated depreciation, D&A $41.9B, unpaid P&E $27.0B), Note 10 Segment Information (net additions by segment), cash flow statement", url:"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1018724/000101872426000004/amzn-20251231.htm"}, {title:"Amazon Q4 2025 earnings release: net sales $716.9B, cash flows, finance-lease additions, P&E acquired but not yet paid", url:"https://s2.q4cdn.com/299287126/files/doc_earnings/2025/q4/earnings-result/AMZN-Q4-2025-Earnings-Release.pdf"}, {title:"Amazon 2024 Form 10-K: prior-year P&E (single 'Equipment' line, no server breakout) and net additions", url:"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1018724/000101872425000004/amzn-20241231.htm"}, {title:"Platformonomics TGIF #115 (Feb 6, 2026): Amazon 2025 capex incl. finance leases $134.74B, AWS $96.5B; Google ~60% servers / 40% data centers and networking", url:"https://platformonomics.com/2026/02/platformonomics-tgif-115-february-6-2026/"}, {title:"Motley Fool (Aug 1, 2025), citing Bloomberg/Barclays estimates: Microsoft 15%, Meta 14%, Alphabet 12%, Amazon 11% of Nvidia revenue", url:"https://www.fool.com/investing/2025/08/01/prediction-nvidia-stock-soar-microsoft-meta"}, {title:"Dell'Oro Group: Data center capex surges 57 percent in 2025 (Top 4 US CSPs +76%)", url:"https://www.delloro.com/news/data-center-capex-surges-57-percent-in-2025-as-ai-deployments-accelerate/"}]},
